  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nProlonged Heat Continues: Latest ensembles are trending towards  \nthe mid level high strengthening just south of the area from \nmid week into the weekend. This will allow for more of a \nwesterly component at 850mb which will allow for even higher \n850mb temps to advect in (24-26C). This would allow for daily \nhighs in the middle/upper 90s east of Springfield, to around 100\nwest of Springfield and would last all the way into the \nweekend. The NBM interquartile spreads are around 2-3 degrees \nwhich allows for higher confidence in these high temps. \n\nThe lack of rainfall and these high temps will continue to allow\nfor vegetation to dry out. This will not only support these \nhigh temps, but will also begin to allow for dewpoints to \nperhaps remain at or below 70 during the day (versus low 70s we\nhave seen recently). While the air temps will increase, lower \ndews may allow for heat indicies to remain generally at or below\n105 in most areas. Indicies around 100 still look likely all \nthe way into the weekend. Therefore extension of heat headlines\nlook likely for Thursday onward. We will need to reassess the \nafternoon mixing potential today and Wednesday for exact \nheadline changes/extensions for future updates. Little relief \nis expected at night with lows in the 70s to around 80. Looking \nat the latest NWS Heat Risk tool (which takes into account how \nunusually above normal temps are, time of year and duration of \nunusual heat), most of the area looks to remain in the major to \nextreme category. While heat indicies themselves may not be \nthat extreme, the duration of the heat (looking to make a run \nat 7+ days in some areas) will put a strain on heat sensitive \ngroups. \n\nDecreasing Precip Chances: Warmer air aloft will make precip\nchances hard to come by into the weekend. Precip chances now\nhave dropped below 20 percent with most places remaining dry.\nThe one area that will need to monitor for precip chances will\nbe the corridor from Morgan county to Phelps county. This area\nwill be just close enough where an isolated shower or storm\ncould drop in. Confidence in this occuring is low. The latest \nCPC outlook suggests an increasing potential for Rapid Onset \nDrought across southwest Missouri going into next week.",
